WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.358
Hi, I'm Crocus!

00:00:02.358 --> 00:00:05.562
Looped transformers reuse one block of layers many times.

00:00:05.562 --> 00:00:09.477
Two families, Ouro and Huginn, differ in three ways at once.

00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:10.545
Which one matters?

00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:15.977
Isolate the three at matched depth, and two wires do almost all

00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:19.454
the work: an untied prelude and coda, and re-showing the loop

00:00:19.454 --> 00:00:20.719
its input every pass.

00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:26.370
Huginn-style models first run tokens through a prelude of eight blocks.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.767
Ouro-style models skip it: the whole network is the loop.

00:00:30.187 --> 00:00:34.511
Then a tied core of twelve blocks runs eight times, and a coda finishes.

00:00:34.511 --> 00:00:37.908
Both designs are matched at a hundred and twelve block executions.

00:00:38.328 --> 00:00:42.162
A write gate re-injects the prelude's output into the core at every

00:00:42.162 --> 00:00:45.037
pass, not just once, through a learned per-channel gate.

00:00:45.457 --> 00:00:49.468
And the loop's starting state is either the input itself, or random

00:00:49.468 --> 00:00:53.479
noise: the move borrowed from equilibrium models, and assumed to be essential.

00:00:54.858 --> 00:00:57.714
Start from Ouro: twenty-eight blocks, looped four times.

00:00:58.134 --> 00:01:03.514
Untie a prelude and coda, and multi-step reasoning jumps: twelve points on MATH500.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:08.928
Re-inject the input every pass, and five benchmarks rise, though math reasoning drops.

00:01:09.348 --> 00:01:12.492
Randomize the starting state, and it's close to a wash.

00:01:12.492 --> 00:01:15.950
The direct start wins six benchmarks of ten, and it's cheaper.

00:01:17.725 --> 00:01:20.413
What a looped model does each pass matters more than how

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.391
its state gets seeded.

00:01:24.858 --> 00:01:28.666
To recap: an untied envelope, the input at every pass, and no

00:01:28.666 --> 00:01:29.935
need for random noise.

00:01:29.935 --> 00:01:33.425
A first look, at small scale, with code still to come.

00:01:33.845 --> 00:01:36.042
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:36.042 --> 00:01:36.670
I'm Crocus.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:36.984
Bye!
